##
    Changing a sequence while walking it.

    This program does not compile. A 'for each' reads its sequence's length once, when the loop
    begins, so inserting into it does not lengthen the walk and removing from it leaves the
    walk reaching past the end. Neither is what the line looks like it does.

    A range loop is the opposite and says so: its bound is read on every turn, so a bound that
    moves is a bound that moves. That is why only 'for each' is refused here.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.Growing();
        Program.Shrinking();
        Program.EmptyingIt();
    end function

    # Inserting. The walk was already measured, so the new element is never reached.
    function Growing()
        integer[] amounts = {1, 2, 3};

        for each amount in amounts
            amounts.Insert(amount * 10);
        end for

        Console.WriteLine(amounts.Count());
    end function

    # Removing. The walk still runs to the length the set had, which is now past its end.
    function Shrinking()
        integer[] amounts = {1, 2, 3};

        for each amount in amounts
            amounts.Remove(amount);
        end for

        Console.WriteLine(amounts.Count());
    end function

    ##
        The same rule reaches a field, and reaches whichever member changes the set — Insert,
        InsertAt, Remove, RemoveAt, and Clear. A member that yields a new set instead leaves
        the original alone and is not in question.
    ##
    function EmptyingIt()
        integer[] amounts = {1, 2, 3};

        for each amount in amounts
            amounts.Clear();
        end for

        Console.WriteLine(amounts.Count());
    end function

end model
